#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153267} :activity/id #uuid "32095b5a-ea4a-5f14-a569-3cb501bb8677" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050150 :topic/id #uuid "3185fbd2-cc78-5eb1-ba66-6a65b74ad8a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2547} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153330 :tutorial/content "Suppose $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\}$ is a set of functions defined on an interval $I\\subseteq R.$\n\n- The set is called **linearly dependent** on $I$ if there exist real numbers $k_1, k_2, \\ldots, k_n,$ *not all zero*, such that\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$  \n\nIntuitively, at least one of the functions can be written as a linear combination of the others.\n\n- The set is called **linearly independent** on $I$ if the *only* way the linear combination\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x)\n$$\n\ncan equal $0$ for all $x \\in I$ is by taking\n\n$$\nk_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that these definitions closely parallel the notions of linear dependence and independence for vectors in a vector space.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set of functions \n\n$$\n\\{ \\sin x, \\, \\cos x, \\, 2\\sin x \\}\n$$\n\ndefined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nNotice that for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot \\sin x +\n0 \\cdot \\cos x +\n(-1) \\cdot 2\\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since not all the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly dependent.\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4d8e0478-c6f1-57b4-b41b-1fe464cb7fdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14499 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3e3b73d3-8c02-5744-a1ae-6c50e8804ed2" :step/math-academy-id 593388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153268}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152277 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151908 :question/id #uuid "708d2d6d-df17-57e3-bff2-ee8809cf9e3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21683"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe4c8bb4-b7c0-5afc-86fd-40c20df9b953" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048257}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421313}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Dependent and Linearly Independent Functions"} :step/id #uuid "be6dde9b-9d0b-585b-a6b3-25eff080cc7b" :step/math-academy-id 593389 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153269}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153328 :tutorial/content "Suppose $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are differentiable functions defined on some interval $I.$ Then, the **Wronskian** is defined as \n![](images/57/57c395afa6eecd7289b91a25e61b5310447cc3702daf019b0046615242f34057.svg)\nand it has the following important properties:\n\nIf $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\nIf $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot deduce any information from $W = 0$ in cases where $y_1$ and $y_2$ are not known to be solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "ede0bd1e-1d97-5029-bbb3-a95bbadc7c88" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14497 :tutorial/title "The Wronskian"} :step/id #uuid "77116319-3453-5eec-ac47-013a9a68819f" :step/math-academy-id 593392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153270}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151905 :question/id #uuid "3da98f86-afab-5b4c-b51a-2ebb42248da1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21680"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050677}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497386}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "ba9f7dc5-2fb4-5f3b-8a80-2aa8c6b90d55" :step/math-academy-id 593393 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152279 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151906 :question/id #uuid "7e72c2e1-8bbd-5047-9394-5df205a9c85a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21681"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432578}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"} :step/id #uuid "25bf33fb-6ffa-53ff-952f-c30347b7e55c" :step/math-academy-id 593397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153329 :tutorial/content "A **fundamental set of solutions** to a homogeneous, linear differential equation of degree $n$ is a set of precisely $n$ linearly independent solutions.\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''(x) - 4y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nUsing direct substitution, it's straightforward to show that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{-2x}$ are the solutions to the equation. \n\nLet's keep track of our solutions by putting them into a set $S,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the Wronskian of our solutions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}){({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{2x})}^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not zero, the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. \n\nBecause the original equation is of degree two, and since the set $S$ contains *precisely* two linearly independent functions, we say that the set $S$ is a fundamental set of solutions to the differential equation.\n\n**Important:** The fundamental set of solutions is not unique. For example, the set $S',$ given by\n\n$$\nS' = \\left\\{4e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis also a fundamental set of solutions. \n\nAs a general rule, we tend to pick the simplest set of linearly independent functions when constructing our fundamental set of solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1733749-f7ac-5c62-80aa-f971e91c66d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14498 :tutorial/title "Sets of Fundamental Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "d45e0175-eb26-56f3-9d0a-ff5fb71127aa" :step/math-academy-id 593400 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151907 :question/id #uuid "c330ca05-3b63-5525-b51e-24d0926a359c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21682"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432580}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "817b1fa5-bf11-5056-811f-2154d660e047" :step/math-academy-id 593401}] :activity/title "Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152305}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432584} :activity/id #uuid "1a21c340-411e-558b-9b34-5988bd8860f9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050150 :topic/id #uuid "3185fbd2-cc78-5eb1-ba66-6a65b74ad8a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2547} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9434ee35-97a1-5285-97bd-642508f58883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474768} {:answer/id #uuid "2e62f471-e501-527c-a2bf-b58c36ed0de8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474769} :answer-field/id #uuid "f52c9065-8b6f-5163-be58-9a1c7fadbe10" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475310} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee5d6baf-53f3-5f60-82f6-8b26db329f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474770} {:answer/id #uuid "87258704-04de-53fe-86be-3991a51c9d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474771}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474771} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f5919f8-4a8b-57fc-ba5e-3d0f046a50c8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475311} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a4f9e18-0783-5aa4-ba7f-a403d553756b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474772} {:answer/id #uuid "b9af53cc-7fb3-530b-a444-9b33c32f55d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474773}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474773} :answer-field/id #uuid "da939024-b939-5a26-a4f7-b9817e50d965" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "248767b0-4e8f-59a2-a18a-6d7a3f7d1129" :question/math-academy-id "q-334262" :question/problem "$$\n4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+6x{y}^{′}-2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n\nIII. The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x).$ Notice that $\\frac{1}{x}={x}^{-1},$ so we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+6x(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-2(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{8}{x}-\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{2}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x).$ Notice that $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{\\frac{1}{2}},$ so we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2})+6x(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2})-2\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ (-{x}^{1/2}+3{x}^{1/2}-2{x}^{1/2}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-1} & {x}^{1/2} \\\\ -{x}^{-2} & \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}-{x}^{1/2}\\cdot (-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2}+{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} :step/id #uuid "209bca2c-7d6d-5544-8ddd-af5776d68180"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a800c5-7e18-59ee-94e7-80cdfd4de19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305356} {:answer/id #uuid "a39ab598-0b8d-5c62-ad42-23748d01375c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306694}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305356} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b09b08-7233-5ed9-8300-197e20e68046" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a221672-bcdc-5f01-bcdf-22405659444f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306315} {:answer/id #uuid "896dce62-f5b3-52c1-8dd8-bc6d2e4c1097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306465} {:answer/id #uuid "a265e633-dc0a-5983-8255-9b4e361560b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306677} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a73cc5-5dc5-51a3-9906-8c4d81c74c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307095} :answer-field/id #uuid "9248dce7-b98e-5ff4-bda2-e01c8b11790b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10fea060-278f-50a3-9b56-183bdf163c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305306} {:answer/id #uuid "d556e851-090a-5509-bf2f-41c8241ee8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307130} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2c59f88-c92c-51fe-84a6-89edef0e2468" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b67df1a9-5674-599d-941a-796b1c85ba70" :question/math-academy-id "q-334118" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x+1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{′} & ({x}^{2})^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(x+1)\\cdot (2x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2x(x+1)-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x \\\\  & =x(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"} :step/id #uuid "5691033c-903f-5c2f-895d-3cf6dc88e9cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bacd32ae-566a-595f-ae61-c7390064179f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474762} {:answer/id #uuid "af9b7df9-824a-5976-8ef0-0ed97e33de24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474763}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474762} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b52e612-ea71-5a2e-a81c-18901ac0011f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475307} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9146691-1ccc-57f9-88b9-54805a3f76b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474764} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb64cb4-e2ff-50e2-a176-9905722d9d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474765} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f934a8-f5e6-5690-a1d4-2746d5ccf5fa" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475308} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c25a7c82-ba97-5324-83d9-8843be6f251d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474766} {:answer/id #uuid "97e7e003-0f8d-59ae-a1f3-79ac064de7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474766} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb9f336-1bf0-5e57-b4b7-e8e56b5843aa" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8005871f-5013-58a1-b9ee-7ef831d491ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-334257" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-12y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nIII. The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{12}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{5}})+2x(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})-12(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x)+2x(3{x}^{2})-12({x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "72926c4d-a224-5762-aec1-fec990efc5e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8de8f1-0aa5-5597-8f2a-04314cbb183e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333095} {:answer/id #uuid "15516e59-127e-5585-b223-c41ec895f21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333176} {:answer/id #uuid "852d9dce-fad7-5d6d-9d52-31ed637d34a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334173} {:answer/id #uuid "8936b9da-7dc8-5edc-ba5b-5f96a52fcf44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334208} {:answer/id #uuid "e05757f5-0bd3-5d05-a456-6e255ec4d863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334942}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334942} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b23fc9c-30a6-50f5-8daf-56d713fe0638" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e41bd3a2-77cd-50f9-af0d-7902e2113a96" :question/math-academy-id "q-64747" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =({e}^{x}){(x{e}^{x})}^{′}-(x{e}^{x}){({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-(x{e}^{x})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}-x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a9c35b23-f5de-5890-a8e5-257e01ed501a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daf94a0c-0e29-57f1-b831-528166c238c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432569} {:answer/id #uuid "dd836c36-70c9-53c0-b7fa-96d4837068ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432569} :answer-field/id #uuid "3672eb02-0003-53d9-90a8-1a9105690abc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432573} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15e9135b-b871-5aba-b40d-ba3025b92b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432557} {:answer/id #uuid "273ff444-7dd9-5e5a-b414-0596922f638a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432560} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5b64e6-9a77-5e45-9c0d-3ad872a7c207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432561} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1519be-802b-5b17-9751-f17a087eadf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432562}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432557} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dbf6a1b-898e-56e9-9f12-6a5e1d80b975" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432575} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab65ccb-a918-513f-ad62-ae4b2896ed85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432558} {:answer/id #uuid "1e50b1fc-9bb0-519a-87b4-b41bfcf588bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432558} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6385f95-6b30-5136-8980-da81fcd668ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10c6f8f6-9fca-5e23-8276-22a855906488" :question/math-academy-id "q-334111" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=2{sin}^{2}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2{sin}^{2}⁡x & 1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {(2{sin}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} & {(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2{sin}^{2}⁡x & 1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2{sin}^{2}⁡x)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =4({sin}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-4({sin}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"} :step/id #uuid "feaa103c-ed4c-5dfc-900e-8d1325c89739" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432582}}] :activity/title "Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432554}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497426} :activity/id #uuid "96b6d663-08d7-585e-8073-b5b5fe80dad2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050150 :topic/id #uuid "3185fbd2-cc78-5eb1-ba66-6a65b74ad8a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2547} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08231897-379f-5465-a685-d58d28a9a029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263074} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdbf991-a81b-541a-86a5-8e9569760048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263403} {:answer/id #uuid "894275a3-764c-5ff1-95dd-588429bf5785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264182} {:answer/id #uuid "90767335-6ee7-503b-aaad-90d180393cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264241} {:answer/id #uuid "edaad585-b008-5187-9422-f08e3851db74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265076}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263074} :answer-field/id #uuid "37094ebd-d0aa-5bec-a937-d45769e3e042" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59cfc6f0-d08d-5f61-ba3e-7216ac027397" :question/math-academy-id "q-129797" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x){(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){(x)}^{′} \\\\  & =(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2155ec54-f705-5c21-80f7-39cc78dc2cd2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497430}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "466436a8-4af6-538b-9c6f-dc9fef31373c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{3x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496811} {:answer/id #uuid "55bfbd25-7cd4-58e5-9f38-dc990ba4a925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496840} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f48d39-9abb-58ad-92f4-b282755cf973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-2{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497093} {:answer/id #uuid "d8339946-1b5f-5196-bce2-e29dceabf2f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497113} {:answer/id #uuid "f715f065-06d5-5837-ae98-a58247ced1f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497187}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497113} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcb0452a-b540-5991-8720-5551a36772ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a6522825-4faa-5281-807b-1fb4ff06c302" :question/math-academy-id "q-129799" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(2{e}^{x}){({e}^{2x})}^{′}-({e}^{2x}){(2{e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =(2{e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{2x})(2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={4}^{3x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "32245bef-db4a-5ead-bf54-75deea3e1bbd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497444}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497434 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcf9011c-88d2-522f-b3b2-7f5e58324603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466165} {:answer/id #uuid "7c54ae2f-cbec-5b96-bc9f-83b395505baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466165} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b76786e-84b5-5680-8436-15f590623f91" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466926} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e52cab96-26d7-57ef-a7bd-1454284967ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466167} {:answer/id #uuid "08e922da-3532-5a0c-84aa-77682d68a282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466168}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466168} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1740d26-3686-55da-8705-422b2c2b00b7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466927} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b152814-ca69-5689-a07f-ea7a68ee13fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466169} {:answer/id #uuid "800df2b3-aa45-578a-a674-f96d56bef770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466169} :answer-field/id #uuid "243b5076-4595-54da-af25-5a1ce4f33b19" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4674804-88cb-5499-be8a-d4a228b08c3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334258" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **II.** ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **III.** The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){e}^{2x} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+5{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "42ed90f3-6e73-597b-a8d4-611af23d26d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497444 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "225a1eff-527f-50a3-a950-0e07356c0c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305479} {:answer/id #uuid "88e4ac3e-acf7-5f50-9fef-7c0d6c5127de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306460}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306460} :answer-field/id #uuid "164c75dd-1d7b-5483-8626-94f363941660" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307590} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4375f462-ef6a-5d9e-bf5b-d24ce61a1925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305816} {:answer/id #uuid "6180adb3-8d26-5a19-9ce0-e694e204c090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306091} {:answer/id #uuid "9f97eab8-1e38-5b7d-963e-d85bc898a662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306647} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e467cf-6110-5dc9-a27d-22ab45048e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305816} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e38717e-3ffb-5a34-9c57-bc6ca0a2c3bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307970} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "129f96e0-7d7f-589c-a331-e0eb85b217cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305314} {:answer/id #uuid "798b8103-db44-5e23-8007-6cac7e843eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306308} :answer-field/id #uuid "b17a5a7a-6c0f-508a-b7cf-5291f1a8c8cc" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5a272db4-485b-515e-b75d-0755cc7e94a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-334109" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}+1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=2+2{x}^{2},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  which is \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2+2{x}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2+2{x}^{2})^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2+2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2x & 4x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)(4x)-(2+2{x}^{2})(2x) \\\\  & =4x({x}^{2}+1)-4x({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"} :step/id #uuid "67570e82-1419-5a80-8fbf-4556faa1f82b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497434}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b216db04-2d87-5f29-b031-4b8f18aebbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466177} {:answer/id #uuid "084e80ea-fab8-56b6-840f-252ff612f881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466178} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d31f03f-7baf-56e7-b1ac-efd300ef99ba" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff9dc928-9b30-5b8d-8c18-90d8a6534ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466179} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb19be9-60e0-5255-a502-12dbdfe06395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466180} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d0b221-8cd2-5d31-84e5-81ec105281e0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466933} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e26dd4-c78f-596b-9c14-f45264b02075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466181} {:answer/id #uuid "c433f3b3-d4f1-587f-a97d-e0085ce49482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466182} :answer-field/id #uuid "98ee50d6-62fb-5851-9ee5-5ce5a71ca168" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fbddaa01-5158-5d3b-b619-76268088aef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334266" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **II.** ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n- **III.** The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2-3x\\cdot 2x+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)-3x(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ 2x & 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} :step/id #uuid "f8efebd5-69c4-553e-b210-51f0453b7375"}] :activity/title "Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496655}]}
